The code explicitly checks for buffer overflow before the memcpy operation:
```c
        if (cp + pdu_data_len > spdu_buf + sizeof(spdu_buf)) {
            snmp_log(LOG_ERR, "%s: PDU too big (\%" NETSNMP_PRIz "d > \%" NETSNMP_PRIz "d)\n",
                     NETSNMP_FUNCTION, pdu_data_len, sizeof(spdu_buf));
            return -1;
        }
        memcpy(cp, pdu_data, pdu_data_len);
```
This `if` statement guards the `memcpy` by comparing the end of the target buffer (`cp + pdu_data_len`) with the maximum allowed end address (`spdu_buf + sizeof(spdu_buf)`). If the `pdu_data_len` would cause an overflow, an error is logged, and the function returns -1, preventing the `memcpy` from being executed with an out-of-bounds size.
